## Worth flagging explicitly

1. **My first fix attempt was itself wrong.** Clipping the backward scan to the nearest `desired`
   step-down at-or-before the *matched drain event's timestamp* fixed the named repro (`r2tnh`) but
   silently regressed 11 correct drain windows to 0 on `m-satta-dwell` -- a cell this spec's own
   verification checklist didn't name, but one I'd used for every prior task's verification and
   re-checked out of habit rather than trusting the single targeted repro. Root cause: the aggregate
   `ready`-poll that produces `drain_events` runs on a coarser cadence than the per-pod scrape and
   can report a decrease *after* a pod's own last sample -- filtering against the drain event's
   timestamp instead of the pod's own last sample let that lag admit a transition that, as of the
   pod's death, hadn't happened yet. Fixed by filtering against the pod's own last sample instead,
   with a fallback to the drain event (capped at the pod's last sample) only when no earlier
   transition exists at all.
2. **The fixed `r2tnh` case shows no window, not a short one.** Your spec's verification section
   said "the fixed window should start close to t≈1073s" -- but `r2tnh`'s last sample (1058s)
   genuinely precedes the real transition (1073s), so no nonzero window can exist there without
   inventing data. Your spec's own step 3 already covers this ("if the clipped window is empty...
   drop it entirely -- not an error"), so I'm treating this as the spec's own fallback firing
   correctly, not a deviation -- but flagging it since the verification wording read like it
   expected a nonzero result.
3. Two other pods (`6l685`, `8xx2f`) lost drain windows they'd shown before this fix. Checked this
   wasn't a new defect: their disappearance coincided with `desired` still climbing toward its peak
   (no real scale-down in progress), so their original match to a nearby `drain_events` entry was
   always a false positive on timing proximity, from the matching step your spec said was already
   correct and out of scope here. Losing them is a side effect of the fix, not something the fix
   itself got wrong.

Full detail, including the exact before/after windows per pod, is in
`session/status/autoscaling-viz.md`, rewritten in place, prior entries preserved below the fold.

A fresh `review__autoscaling-viz-drain-window-fix-ready.md` trigger is filed for `/code-review`,
flagging both points above for the reviewer's own independent check.

